<name><surname>Karamitsos</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Economidou</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Dantis</surname><given-names>PC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Serum IL-6, TNF<italic>&#x003b1;</italic>, p55 srTNF<italic>&#x003b1;</italic>, p75 srTNF<italic>&#x003b1;</italic>, srIL-2<italic>&#x003b1;</italic> levels and disease activity in systemic lupus erythematosus</article-title><source><italic>Clinical Rheumatology</italic></source><year>1999</year><volume>18</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>22</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10088943</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B8"><label>8</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sabry</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Sheashaa</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>El-Husseini</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-<italic>&#x003b1;</italic> and IL-6) in Egyptian patients with SLE: its correlation with disease activity</article-title><source><italic>Cytokine</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>35</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>148</fpage><lpage>153</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17027280</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B9"><label>9</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gabay</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Cakir</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Moral</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Circulating levels of tumor necrosis factor soluble receptors in systemic lupus erythematosus are significantly higher than in other rheumatic diseases and correlate with disease activity</article-title><source><italic>The Journal of Rheumatology</italic></source><year>19